{
  "title": "The Bioprocess Brief",
  "research_profile": "I study how mitochondrial biogenesis and energy metabolism driven by nuclear respiratory factor 1 (NRF1) supply ATP to the endoplasmic reticulum through the AXER/SLC35B1 ATP/ADP exchanger, and whether engineering this axis raises secretory capacity and recombinant protein productivity in CHO cells. Most useful to me: ER ATP import or ER energy status; NRF1/PGC-1/TFAM-driven mitochondrial biogenesis in mammalian cells; energy or redox engineering (NDI1, AOX, LbNOX, NAD+ transport) in production cells; genetically encoded ATP or redox biosensors usable in CHO; and CHO cell line or bioprocess studies linking metabolism to specific productivity. Treat NRF1 as nuclear respiratory factor 1, not NFE2L1, unless the paper says otherwise; GABPA (NRF2 in the mitochondrial sense) is distinct from NFE2L2.",
  "interest_labels": [
    "Bioprocessing",
    "Mammalian cell engineering",
    "Metabolic engineering",
    "Cell & gene therapy",
    "CHO production systems",
    "Mitochondrial regulation & ER ATP",
    "CHO secretory productivity & biosensors"
  ],
  "interests": [
    "Bioprocess development, process intensification, scale-up, perfusion, media optimization, and process analytics",
    "Mammalian cell engineering, especially Chinese hamster ovary (CHO) cell line development and recombinant protein production",
    "Metabolic engineering, flux analysis, and systems biology with a clear application to industrial biotechnology or mammalian cells",
    "Cell and gene therapy manufacturing, viral vector production, engineered therapeutic cells, and enabling platform technologies",
    "The mechanistic link from nuclear respiratory factor 1 (NRF1)-driven mitochondrial biogenesis and energy metabolism through AXER/SLC35B1-mediated endoplasmic reticulum ATP supply to CHO secretory capacity and recombinant protein productivity, including energy/redox engineering and biosensors. Distinguish nuclear respiratory factor 1 from the NFE2L1 alias NRF1, and GABPA (nuclear respiratory factor 2 in mitochondrial literature) from NFE2L2/NRF2; flag ambiguous gene identities rather than assuming equivalence."
  ],
